# NativeBlade {{VERSION}}: context for AI coding agents

Written by `nativeblade:install` and rewritten by `nativeblade:update`, so it always describes the version installed in this project. Do not edit it; it is replaced on the next update. For anything not covered here, ask the `nativeblade` MCP server (`list_docs`, `read_doc`, `architecture_recipe`, `list_facade_methods`, `describe_facade_method`, `project_state`), which answers from this same installed version.

## What this project is

A Laravel + Livewire app that ships as a native mobile and desktop app. PHP runs inside the app, in php-wasm, behind a native shell (Tauri). There is no server: every request is served on the device, one at a time, from the bundle built out of this project. Read the MCP document `ARCHITECTURE.md` before planning or building a feature: one Livewire component per screen, thin components with services, and the anti-patterns it lists.

## How PHP runs on the device

- Native work (an `Http::` request, a query on the native database, an operation on a native disk) pauses PHP while the shell does it and resumes with the result. A request runs once, top to bottom, as on a server.
- Where the WebView supports JSPI (Android, Windows, Linux) any PHP stack can be paused. On iOS and macOS the Asyncify build runs and only instrumented paths can; pausing anywhere else ends the PHP instance with "memory access out of bounds" or runs the request twice. The rules below exist for that build and apply everywhere so the app behaves the same on every platform.
- Never wait inside PHP: no `sleep`, `usleep`, `Illuminate\Support\Sleep`, `Http::retry()` with a delay (a delay of 0 is fine), `Cache::lock()->block()`, `LazyCollection::throttle()`, or the `retry()` helper with a delay. A wait belongs in the user interface.
- No sockets, processes or curl: `fsockopen`, `file_get_contents` on a URL, `proc_open`, `exec`, the `Process` facade, `curl_exec`, queue workers and cron do not exist on the device. Network goes through `Http::`, which the shell runs natively. Several requests at once go through `NativeBlade::pool()`.
- A native call must not happen inside the callback of `usort`, `uasort`, `uksort`, `array_walk`, `json_encode` (a `JsonSerializable`), `count()` (a `Countable`), `preg_replace_callback`, an output buffer handler, a user error handler or an autoloader. Collect the data first, then sort, encode or walk it. Doing so throws `NativeCallNotAllowed` on every platform, PHPUnit included. `array_map`, `array_filter`, `array_reduce`, `collect()->map()`, generators and magic methods are fine.
- `php artisan nativeblade:doctor` scans the app (and the packages added under `vendor`) for all of the above and prints file and line. Run it before committing a turn that touched PHP and fix every item; exit code 1 means the app would crash on a phone.
- A crash that still happens is reported in the dev server terminal and the WebView console as a line starting with "PHP crashed". It names the native call that was in flight, or php-wasm's own suspension points to look for, and the shell reloads the app on its own.

## Working in this project

- Logic lives in PHP. State, rules, validation, persistence and navigation belong to the Livewire component; the view uses `wire:click`, `wire:model` and `wire:nb-navigate`. Write JavaScript only for what Livewire cannot express well (a gesture, a drag, a canvas, a tight game loop, a third-party widget), keep it small, and let PHP stay the only owner of the data: JS reports to PHP through Livewire and never keeps its own copy of the state.
- `php artisan nativeblade:dev` serves the app with hot reload for Blade, PHP and CSS (Tailwind is recompiled on every save and pushed into the running app). Do not run `npm run build` for styles.
- App configuration (name, identifier, version, permissions, plugins, platform options) lives in `app/Providers/AppServiceProvider.php`, in the `NativeBladeConfig::desktop()`, `android()` and `ios()` blocks. After changing it, run `php artisan nativeblade:config`; `nativeblade:dev` and `nativeblade:build` run it for you.
- The version is `->version('x.y.z', build)` in each platform block. Bump the semver string and the integer build number together, on every platform block present, for every change that ships.
- `php artisan nativeblade:icon` generates every platform icon from a 1024x1024 PNG at `src-tauri/icons/logo.png`.
- `NativeBlade::log($message, $context, $level)` writes to the WebView console, the dev server terminal and `nativeblade.log` on the device; `php artisan nativeblade:logs --platform=desktop|android|ios` reads that file. PHP warnings and fatals reach the same places.
- In PHPUnit, `NativeBlade::fake()` stands in for the shell: it records native calls, serves an in-memory SQLite for the native database and asserts on actions and HTTP calls. The suspend guard runs inside it, so a native call from an unpausable callback fails in tests too.
- The app's chrome and overlays are the app's own: top bar, bottom tabs, side drawer, modals, sheets and toasts are Blade and CSS in the layout and views, with their state in the Livewire component. Do not use the shell components `<x-nativeblade-header>`, `<x-nativeblade-bottom-nav>`, `<x-nativeblade-tab>`, `<x-nativeblade-drawer>`, `<x-nativeblade-drawer-item>` and `<x-nativeblade-modal>`; they are legacy and will be removed. The shell already pads the body for the notch and the home indicator, so a sticky `top: 0` header and a sticky `bottom: 0` tab bar sit at the safe edges; `--nb-safe-*` exists for the rare fixed element that needs more. A plain yes/no question is a native dialog: `->confirm()` or `->alert()` on the NativeBlade response.
- Icons: `<x-nativeblade-icon name="heart" />` renders the Phosphor set; `heart-bold` and `heart-fill` are the other weights. `<x-nativeblade-skeleton>` and `<x-nativeblade-image>` are the other built-in components.
- Native features (camera, push, files, sensors, payments, sharing, biometrics and the rest) are plugins declared in the provider and used through the `NativeBlade` facade; each has an MCP doc page. Do not guess a facade method: call `list_facade_methods` and `describe_facade_method`.
